## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a common real-world challenge in Kafka streaming: handling evolving event schemas. It tests understanding of versioned schemas, backward/forward compatibility, data transformation, multi-sink routing with threshold-based logic, and idempotent sinks with per-message deduplication.

## Key Concepts
- **Versioned schemas and backward/forward compatibility**: Handling schema evolution without breaking consumers
- **Data transformation and normalization**: Converting multiple schema versions into a unified structure
- **Idempotent sinks and per-message deduplication**: Ensuring exactly-once semantics using deduplication
- **Multi-sink routing**: Directing events to different destinations based on business logic

## Code Example
```javascript
class VersionedConsumer {
  constructor(threshold, sinks) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.sinks = sinks;
    this.seenIds = new LRUCache(10000);
  }

  process(message) {
    const msgId = message.messageId;
    if (this.seenIds.has(msgId)) return; // skip duplicates

    const record = this.normalize(message);
    const sink = record.value > this.threshold ? this.sinks.alerts : this.sinks.default;
    sink.write(record);
    this.seenIds.add(msgId);
  }

  normalize(msg) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(msg.payload);
    return {
      sensorId: obj.sensorId,
      value: obj.value,
      timestamp: obj.timestamp,
      location: obj.version === 2 ? obj.location : null,
      version: obj.version
    };
  }
}
```

